    "description": "# Future Directions\n\n## 1. Locally uniform joint removal of prime and occupation cutoffs\n\n**Conjecture.** On every compact subset of the half-plane `Re(s) > 1`, finite\nprime occupation products converge uniformly to the Riemann zeta function when\nthe prime cutoff and occupation ceiling tend jointly to infinity.  The error\nadmits a uniform split into an omitted-prime contribution and the additive\noccupation-tail contribution established by the finite model.\n\nThe key insight is that the occupation error is already controlled independently\nof the omitted modes, so the two limits can be scheduled using separate majorants.\n\n**Why now?** The finite geometric defect has an exact factorization and an\nadditive upper bound; only a compact-uniform prime-tail estimate remains to join\nit to the classical Euler product.\n\n## 2. Asymptotic sharpness and the least retained prime\n\n**Conjecture.** For a fixed nonempty finite prime set and positive inverse\ntemperature, the normalized occupation defect is asymptotic to the sum of the\nlargest local tails.  In particular, when the least retained prime is unique,\nthe defect divided by its omitted occupation weight tends to one.\n\nThe key insight is that products of two or more local tails decay faster than\nthe dominant single-tail term in the inclusion\u2013exclusion expansion.\n\n**Why now?** Exact calculations show the additive union bound rapidly becoming\nsharp, and the finite defect identity exposes all correction terms explicitly.\n\n## 3. Complex-temperature occupation bounds\n\n**Conjecture.** For complex inverse temperature in any closed half-strip\n`Re(s) \u2265 1 + \u03b4`, the finite occupation defect satisfies a modulus bound with the\nsame geometric decay rate obtained by replacing each local weight by its\nabsolute value.\n\nThe key insight is that the algebraic defect identity remains valid over the\ncomplex numbers, while absolute values reduce its quantitative control to the\nnonnegative real estimate.\n\n**Why now?** The real-weight theorem identifies the exact inequality that must be\ntransported through the complex norm, providing a direct route to locally\nuniform analytic convergence.\n\n## 4. Archimedean completion as a distinguished boundary mode\n\n**Conjecture.** A positive integral occupation sector can realize the gamma\nfactor of the completed zeta function, and its coupling to the prime sectors can\nbe chosen so that Mellin duality acts as the reflection `s \u21a6 1-s`.\n\nThe key insight is that nonarchimedean truncation errors are now isolated and\ncontrolled, leaving the local factor at infinity as a separate construction\nrather than an unexplained correction.\n\n**Why now?** The finite prime model supplies a stable multiplicative core and a\nprecise error budget against which an archimedean sector can be tested.\n\n## 5. Response positivity and zero-free regions\n\n**Conjecture.** After archimedean completion, a renormalized quadratic response\nform derived from logarithmic occupation fluctuations is positive on a specified\ntest-function cone exactly when the completed zeta function has no zeros in the\ncorresponding symmetric pair of half-planes.\n\nThe key insight is that a meaningful stability criterion must encode zeros\nthrough an explicit spectral or explicit-formula decomposition, rather than\nthrough positivity of the elementary local energies alone.\n\n**Why now?** Finite occupation stability and cutoff errors are quantitatively\nunderstood, sharply separating harmless local positivity from the genuinely\nglobal spectral term that such a criterion must detect.\n",
