    "description": "# Future directions\n\n## What is proved here\n\nThe Lean development establishes a reusable discrete CRN semantics and an exact compiler for deterministic finite-state transition systems. Each state is represented by one molecule, each transition by a unary reaction, and arbitrary finite traces are simulated exactly while total molecular count remains one.\n\nIt also proves an end-to-end preparation-cost theorem. In the stated model, preparing `n` molecular candidates costs `p*n`, testing all candidates takes one parallel round, and `p \u2265 1`. Sequential search then costs at most twice molecular search, including candidate spaces of size `2^k`.\n\nFinally, it formalizes an assumption-explicit information-capacity lower bound: if complexity `k` fits in volume `v` at `b` bits per volume unit, then `k \u2264 b*v`, equivalently `ceil(k/b) \u2264 v` when `b > 0`.\n\n## Next formal steps\n\n1. **Autonomous CRN execution.** Replace the externally scheduled `runCompiled` semantics with a transition relation over reaction multisets, then prove that exactly one compiled reaction is enabled at every reachable one-hot population.\n2. **Bounded Turing machines.** Define tapes of bounded length and compile complete machine configurations into species. The finite-state compiler in this directory should then yield exact simulation up to the tape bound.\n3. **Unbounded universality.** Add a representation of counters or tapes by molecular counts and prove simulation of a known universal register machine. This is the substantial missing step toward a genuine CRN Turing-completeness theorem.\n4. **Mass-action kinetics.** Connect discrete firings to stochastic or deterministic mass-action semantics. Exact trajectory simulation will require a carefully stated encoding and observation theorem; ordinary differential-equation trajectories should not be conflated with discrete reaction traces.\n5. **Kolmogorov complexity.** Fix a universal description language and a compiler from descriptions to CRNs. Prove lower bounds from finite encoding capacity and upper bounds from compiler size. The current file proves only the generic capacity implication, not proportionality of physical volume and Kolmogorov complexity.\n6. **Preparation-aware complexity models.** Generalize the linear preparation model to bounded-rate synthesis, copying, routing, and readout. Identify assumptions under which constant-factor, polynomial, or larger speedups are possible.\n7. **NP search.** Formalize candidate generation and verification separately. The present theorem applies to exhaustive candidate preparation; it does not prove a complexity-theoretic lower bound for every possible molecular algorithm.\n8. **Empirical DNA claims.** Storage density (`10^18` bits per cubic micrometer) and throughput (`10^15` operations/second) need experimentally justified parameters and unit-aware physical models. They are deliberately not asserted as theorems here.\n\n## Scope warning\n\nThe broad claim that mass-action CRNs are Turing-complete depends strongly on the precise CRN semantics, admissible concentrations/counts, error model, and output convention. Likewise, a universal physical-volume/Kolmogorov proportionality theorem is false without encoding-capacity and compiler assumptions. Future statements should retain these assumptions explicitly.\n",
