    "description": "# Future Directions\n\n## 1. Locally finite orbit-triangular decomposition on the Tits cone\n\n**Conjecture.** For every symmetrizable Kac\u2013Moody root datum and twisting weight, the shifted Chinta\u2013Gunnells averages form a topological Schauder basis of the invariant holomorphic functions on every Weyl-stable compact subset of the interior of the complexified Tits cone, when coefficients are ordered by dominance.\n\nThe key insight is that the finite orbit-indicator decomposition should persist as a locally finite triangular expansion once orbit support is replaced by dominance-controlled analytic support. **Why now?** The finite theorem isolates uniqueness from analytic continuation, making the exact convergence statement that remains to be established both visible and testable.\n\n## 2. Dominance graph criterion beyond simply-laced type\n\n**Conjecture.** For every symmetrizable generalized Cartan matrix, the dominant labels occurring in the decomposition admit a finite collection of weighted-degree inequalities on the oriented multiple-bond Dynkin graph, generalizing the simply-laced criterion `degree(i) + degree_D(i) \u2265 2`.\n\nThe key insight is that coroot pairings turn root sums into weighted in-degrees, so non-simply-laced dominance should remain local after edge multiplicities and symmetrizers are incorporated. **Why now?** A complete simply-laced criterion is available as an anchor, and the weighted formulation can be tested immediately on rank-two finite and affine matrices.\n\n## 3. Hecke-idempotent interpretation of twisted averaging\n\n**Conjecture.** After localization away from the metaplectic denominator, the twisted Chinta\u2013Gunnells averaging operator is an idempotent in a completed unequal-parameter Hecke algebra, and its image is exactly the space appearing in the decomposition theorem.\n\nThe key insight is that finite Reynolds projection and twisted Weyl averaging share the same projection geometry, while the cocycle should deform the group algebra into a Hecke-type completion. **Why now?** The finite idempotence theorem identifies the algebraic identity that the localized cocycle must satisfy and separates it from all convergence questions.\n\n## 4. Affine extra functional equations from diagram-lattice symmetry\n\n**Conjecture.** In affine type, every functional equation of a fundamental-weight-twisted average that does not arise from the affine Weyl group is induced by an automorphism of the extended Dynkin diagram combined with a translation in the coweight lattice; in type `A\u0303\u2081`, this mechanism generates all extra equations.\n\nThe key insight is that the unexplained equations should be symmetries of the extended root datum rather than accidental identities of rational functions. **Why now?** The explicit `A\u0303\u2081` equations provide a minimal falsifiable test, while the orbit viewpoint predicts precisely how such a symmetry must permute decomposition coefficients.\n\n## 5. Function-field coefficient positivity and crystal multiplicities\n\n**Conjecture.** After a uniform power-of-`q` renormalization, every coefficient in the dominant-weight decomposition of the rational-function-field multiple Dirichlet series is a polynomial in `q` with nonnegative integer coefficients, and its value at `q = 1` is a weight multiplicity in the highest-weight module determined by the twisting parameter.\n\nThe key insight is that dominant labels already come from a highest-weight module, suggesting that arithmetic coefficients refine crystal multiplicities rather than merely being indexed by them. **Why now?** The decomposition theorem supplies canonical coefficients, so positivity can be tested in low-rank finite and affine examples without ambiguity about normalization or basis choice.\n",
