    "consumed_by_exp_id": "",
    "description": "# Future directions\n\n## 1. Arithmetic truth as an unbounded limit of bounded truth tables\n\n**Conjecture.** For a concrete syntax of first-order arithmetic with a standard-model satisfaction relation, every length-bounded fragment has a finite exact truth table, but there is no single computable procedure whose restrictions produce all of these tables uniformly.\n\nThe key insight is that each finite stage is harmless while uniformity across all stages can encode the full unbounded truth problem.\n\n**Why now?** Exact finite tabulation and diagonal non-enumeration have been separated cleanly, making the missing uniformity condition precise enough to target by a reduction.\n\n## 2. Quantitative advice lower bounds for bounded semantics\n\n**Conjecture.** For binary statements of length `n`, any family of predictors that realizes every Boolean semantics exactly requires at least `2^n` bits of worst-case advice; under a 95-percent Hamming-accuracy requirement, the optimal advice length is governed asymptotically by the volume of radius-0.05 Hamming balls in the Boolean cube.\n\nThe key insight is that finite computability does not remove information-theoretic storage barriers, and approximate prediction converts truth tables into a covering-code problem.\n\n**Why now?** The exact table theorem identifies the ambient Boolean cube, while the adversarial theorem shows that semantics-free guarantees need additional information.\n\n## 3. Sound abstention under a coverage constraint\n\n**Conjecture.** If definite answers must always be sound and an oracle must answer on at least a fixed positive fraction of every sufficiently rich arithmetic benchmark, then no computable oracle attains the requirement uniformly over all length bounds.\n\nThe key insight is that abstention ceases to be vacuous only when soundness is paired with an explicit lower bound on coverage.\n\n**Why now?** The three-valued model already isolates abstention and counts it as failure, so alternative precision-and-coverage scoring rules can be compared without ambiguity.\n\n## 4. A concrete relativized jump and strict degree hierarchy\n\n**Conjecture.** A partial-recursive oracle-machine model admits a concrete jump operator whose iterates form a strict order embedding of the natural numbers into the Turing degrees.\n\nThe key insight is that diagonal complementation supplies the combinatorial obstruction, while a universal relativized machine supplies the effective coding needed to turn that obstruction into reducibility statements.\n\n**Why now?** The sequence-level diagonal theorem and the existing abstract strict jump hierarchy delimit exactly what must be added: a universal machine, relativized simulation, and the halting reduction.\n\n## 5. Distribution-sensitive limits for bounded truth prediction\n\n**Conjecture.** Under any computably samplable sequence of full-support distributions on arithmetic sentences whose lengths tend to infinity, no computable predictor is uniformly 95-percent accurate unless the distributions concentrate on a computably decidable semantic fragment.\n\nThe key insight is that meaningful average-case accuracy depends jointly on semantic hardness and on how probability mass moves through increasingly expressive fragments.\n\n**Why now?** Pointwise exactness on finite domains has ruled out bare fixed-length impossibility, leaving distributional uniformity as the natural testable replacement.\n",
