    "description": "# Future Directions: Sharpness of Prime-to-Characteristic Averaging\n\n## Contrarian conjecture tested\n\nA tempting strengthening of the finite-group averaging theorem is:\n\n> **Unconditional averaging conjecture.** Every degree-one cocycle of a finite\n> group acting linearly on a field-vector space is a coboundary, even when the\n> group order is zero in the coefficient field.\n\nThis conjecture is **disproved uniformly in every prime characteristic** in\n`Catalog/Pythagorean/GreenbergMuZeroSharpness.lean`.\n\nFor a prime `p`, let\n\n- `G = Multiplicative (ZMod p)`, the additive cyclic group of order `p` written\n  multiplicatively;\n- `V = ZMod p`;\n- every element of `G` act trivially on `V`; and\n- `c(g)` be the underlying additive coordinate of `g`.\n\nThen `c(gh) = c(g) + g \u00b7 c(h)`, so `c` is a degree-one cocycle. Every coboundary\nfor the trivial action is identically zero, whereas `c(1) = 1 \u2260 0`. Thus `c` is\nnot a coboundary. Moreover, the cardinality of `G`, cast into `ZMod p`, is zero.\nThis proves that the invertibility hypothesis in finite averaging marks a real\nboundary, rather than an artifact of the usual proof.\n\n## Machine-checked results\n\nThe Lean file proves:\n\n1. `identityCocycle_cocycle`: the additive coordinate satisfies the cocycle law\n   for the trivial action.\n2. `identityCocycle_not_coboundary`: for every prime `p`, this cocycle is not a\n   coboundary.\n3. `counterexample_to_unconditional_averaging`: the group order vanishes in the\n   coefficient field while both preceding properties hold.\n\nAll three theorems compile independently without `sorry` or additional axioms.\n\n## Evidence-stage justification\n\nA separate computational-evidence artifact was not produced because the Lean\nresult is a symbolic theorem for every prime, not an inference from sampled\ninstances or a conjectured integer sequence. The familiar small cases `p = 2,\n3, 5, 7` are direct specializations of the proved theorem; there is no associated\nOEIS sequence or numerical plot that would add evidence beyond the proof itself.\nKeeping this justification here respects the requested output restriction to\nLean files and `FUTURE_DIRECTIONS.md`.\n\n## Next formal steps\n\nThe counterexample identifies the exact obstruction that a profinite theorem\nmust exclude. Productive next steps are:\n\n- define continuous crossed homomorphisms into finite discrete modules and prove\n  that each factors through a finite quotient;\n- transfer finite averaging across that quotient when every quotient order is\n  prime to the coefficient characteristic;\n- contrast this with pro-`p` quotients, where the family above supplies finite\n  nonvanishing classes;\n- formulate the resulting degree-one vanishing theorem as the first continuous\n  cohomology input to the Selmer and deformation-theoretic developments.\n",
