    "description": "# Future Directions \u2014 Parity-Sensitive Stability of Optimal Hamilton Covers\n\n## Cycle-2 advance\n\nThe local part of the proposed near-optimal stability conjecture is true in a\nstrictly sharper form.\n\nLet a target graph have maximum degree `\u0394`, let a family of two-regular spanning\nlayers be contained in and cover its edge set, and suppose the family has\n\n\\[\n  m=\\left\\lceil\\frac{\\Delta}{2}\\right\\rceil+k\n\\]\n\nlayers. At every maximum-degree vertex, define repeated incidence multiplicity\nas the sum of its incidences over all layers minus the number of distinct\nincidences in their union. Then\n\n\\[\n  \\operatorname{rep}(v)=2k+(\\Delta\\bmod 2).\n\\]\n\nConsequently the proposed bound `rep(v) \u2264 2k + 1` holds. More precisely:\n\n* if `\u0394` is even, `rep(v) = 2k`;\n* if `\u0394` is odd, `rep(v) = 2k + 1`, so the advertised upper bound is attained.\n\nThese statements are proved in\n`Catalog/Physics/HamiltonCover/Stability.lean`. The formalization also proves\nthat containment and coverage identify the union of all layer-incidence blocks\nwith the target incidence set. Thus the subtraction really counts repeated\nedge-layer incidences.\n\n## What was proved\n\n1. **Union identification.** For layers contained in the target and covering it,\n   the union of the incident-edge sets at any vertex equals the target's\n   incident-edge set.\n2. **Exact stability law.** At a target-degree-`\u0394` vertex in a cover with\n   `\u2308\u0394/2\u2309 + k` two-regular layers, repeated multiplicity is exactly\n   `2k + \u0394 % 2`.\n3. **The stated local bound.** Repeated multiplicity is at most `2k + 1`.\n4. **Parity refinement.** The bound improves to `2k` for even `\u0394` and is exact\n   for odd `\u0394`.\n\nNo part of the pseudorandom global `O(kn)` edit-distance assertion is claimed:\nit requires a precise edit metric and quantitative pseudorandom hypotheses.\n\n## Contrarian consequences\n\nThe exact identity rules out two tempting strengthenings:\n\n* **False strengthening:** repeated multiplicity is always at most `2k`.\n  This fails whenever `\u0394` is odd; the formal odd-degree theorem forces\n  `2k + 1`.\n* **False interpretation:** the extra `+1` is merely proof slack. It is a\n  necessary parity term, not an artifact of an inequality argument.\n\nThe result also shows that pseudorandomness cannot improve the local formula:\nall graph structure disappears after the cover and two-regularity assumptions\nare imposed. Pseudorandomness can only enter the global repair step.\n\n## Bold next conjectures\n\n### 1. Slack-to-switching conjecture\n\nFor a quantitatively specified pseudorandom graph and a two-regular cover with\n`\u2308\u0394/2\u2309 + k` layers, the exact local repeated-incidence budget can be routed by\nalternating switches so that at most `Ckn` edge-layer incidences are changed to\nobtain an optimal cover, for an absolute constant `C`.\n\nThe new identity supplies the correct conservation law for a switching proof:\nthe total removable local load is parity plus exactly two units per excess\nlayer. The missing ingredient is a simultaneous switching lemma that prevents\nlocal repairs from colliding.\n\n### 2. Even-degree one-step elimination conjecture\n\nIn a robust expander of even maximum degree, every non-optimal two-regular cover\nwith one excess layer admits a bounded-length alternating switching system that\nremoves that layer while preserving coverage.\n\nEven degree is the clean test case because the formal result gives exactly two\nrepeated incidences per maximum-degree vertex and no parity residue.\n\n### 3. Odd-degree defect-matching conjecture\n\nIn a robust expander of odd maximum degree, the unavoidable one-unit defects at\nmaximum-degree vertices can be paired by edge-disjoint alternating paths, after\nwhich every two units of remaining repeated incidence eliminate one excess\nlayer.\n\nThis isolates the parity defect as a matching/routing object rather than\nabsorbing it into an error term.\n\n### 4. Nonuniform-degree transport conjecture\n\nFor a near-regular pseudorandom graph, define the local capacity slack\n\n\\[\n  s(v)=2m-d(v).\n\\]\n\nThere is a flow on an auxiliary switching network that transports all slack\nabove the unavoidable parity residues to a set of `O(kn)` repair operations.\nThis would extend the maximum-degree identity to the vertices where the target\ndegree is below `\u0394` and explain how local surplus becomes global edit distance.\n\n## Evidence-stage decision\n\nA separate finite computation was not used. The advanced claim is an exact\nidentity for arbitrary natural `\u0394` and `k`, and the Lean proof is symbolic and\nuniversal; small-case enumeration would be strictly weaker and there is no\nassociated sequence requiring an OEIS lookup. Parity cases are captured by the\nproved even- and odd-degree theorems themselves.\n",
