    "consumed_by_exp_id": "",
    "description": "# Future Directions\n\nThe present development establishes a finite, discrete chain:\n\n1. a pointwise code-length bound on information density;\n2. mutual information bounded by expected description length;\n3. monotonicity of the square-root PAC-Bayes radius;\n4. transfer of an information-theoretic generalization guarantee to expected and uniform description-length bounds;\n5. monotonic improvement of the resulting guarantee as descriptions become shorter.\n\nNatural next steps are:\n\n- **Derive the PAC-Bayes premise probabilistically.** Formalize the exponential-moment and change-of-measure argument that produces the assumed mutual-information gap bound with a confidence penalty.\n- **Allow zero-probability events.** Replace strict positivity by absolute continuity and use an extended-real-valued KL divergence, including the standard endpoint conventions.\n- **Connect to Mathlib probability spaces.** Express the joint law as the distribution of random variables `S` and `H`, and prove equivalence with measure-theoretic mutual information.\n- **Formalize prefix-free compression.** Introduce binary codewords, prove Kraft's inequality, and derive suitable pointwise or expected information-density bounds from code lengths.\n- **Treat randomized and deterministic learners explicitly.** Build a joint law from a data distribution and a Markov kernel, then specialize to deterministic algorithms.\n- **Prove expectation and high-probability variants.** Relate expected generalization bounds involving `I(S;H)` to PAC-Bayes bounds holding with probability at least `1 - \u03b4`.\n- **Obtain strict improvement criteria.** Under positivity and nondegeneracy assumptions, strengthen no-worse monotonicity to a strictly smaller radius when description length strictly decreases.\n- **Extend beyond finite spaces.** Develop countable and standard Borel versions using Radon\u2013Nikodym derivatives and measure-theoretic relative entropy.\n",
