    "description": "# Future directions\n\n## State of the development\n\nEvery Lean file in the repository compiles end-to-end against Mathlib (`lake\nbuild` from the repository root builds all declared libraries), and no file in\n`Catalog/` contains a `sorry` or an `admit`.  The mission files use no axioms\nbeyond `propext`, `Classical.choice` and `Quot.sound`.  See `BUILD_STATUS.md`\nfor the state of the unrelated `Shared/` scratch library, which was repaired as\npart of this pass.  The mission files are:\n\n* `Catalog/Cryptography/IsogenySIDH/KaniLemma.lean` \u2014 Kani's lemma in an\n  abstract model of isogenies (abelian groups of points, group homomorphisms,\n  dual isogenies given by their two degree relations, surjectivity of isogenies\n  over an algebraically closed field), together with a concrete non-vacuity\n  witness coming from complex multiplication by `\u2124[i]`.  The concrete side is\n  now complete: the `n`-torsion of `\u211a/\u2124` is computed to have exactly `n`\n  elements (`card_nTorsion_QZ`, via the exact sequence `0 \u2192 n\u2124 \u2192 \u2124 \u2192 (\u211a/\u2124)[n]\n  \u2192 0` induced by `k \u21a6 k/n`), the `\u2124[i]`-action on `(\u211a/\u2124)\u00b2` is shown faithful\n  (`gaussEnd_injective`), and consequently the Kani isogeny of the explicit\n  `(5,2)`-diamond has kernel of order `N\u00b2 = 49` (`cmDiamond_card_ker`).\n* `Catalog/Cryptography/IsogenySIDH/RadicalMontgomery.lean` and\n  `DeepRadicalMontgomery.lean` \u2014 correctness and exact fibers of the radical\n  Montgomery `2`-isogeny formulas.\n\nNatural continuations, roughly in order of increasing difficulty.\n\n## 1. Replace the abstract model by Mathlib's elliptic curves\n\nMathlib has Weierstrass curves, their group law, and division polynomials, but\nno theory of isogenies, dual isogenies, or degrees. The `Diamond` structure\nused here isolates exactly the interface that such a theory must provide:\n\n* `\u03c6^ \u2218 \u03c6 = [deg \u03c6]` and `\u03c6 \u2218 \u03c6^ = [deg \u03c6]`;\n* surjectivity of a nonzero isogeny on geometric points.\n\nProviding this interface for `WeierstrassCurve.Affine.Point` over an\nalgebraically closed field would immediately transport every theorem in this\nfile to genuine elliptic curves. The intermediate steps are: V\u00e9lu's formulas\nfor the quotient by a finite subgroup, multiplicativity of the degree, and\nexistence/uniqueness of the dual.\n\n## 2. `#E[N] = N\u00b2` and the exact degree of the Kani isogeny\n\n`card_ker_kani` reduces the degree computation `deg F = N\u00b2` to the statement\n`#E\u2082[N] = N\u00b2`, i.e. `E[N] \u2245 (\u2124/N)\u00b2` for `N` prime to the characteristic.  For\nthe CM torus this is now proved here (`card_nTorsion_QZ` together with\n`card_nTorsion_prod` gives `#((\u211a/\u2124)\u00b2)[N] = N\u00b2`, whence `cmDiamond_card_ker`).\nFor genuine elliptic curves over a field the corresponding statement is a\nclassical result still absent from Mathlib; it would be a valuable standalone\naddition (it also feeds the Weil pairing, already partially present in the\ncatalog).\n\n## 3. The Castryck\u2013Decru attack as an algorithm\n\nThe present file proves the *structural* half of the attack: the SIDH public\ndata determines a subgroup of `E\u2081 \u00d7 E\u2084` which is exactly the kernel of an\n`(N,N)`-isogeny of abelian surfaces (`mem_ker_kani_iff`), and this kernel is\nthe graph of an isomorphism `E\u2081[N] \u2243 E\u2084[N]` (`exists_unique_partner_left` and\n`glueMap_mem_ker`). Two further steps make it an algorithm:\n\n* **Smoothness/decomposition.** When `N = 2^k` (or a product of small primes)\n  the `(N,N)`-isogeny factors into `k` steps of `(2,2)`-isogenies, computable\n  by Richelot's formulas. Formalizing Richelot correspondences on genus-2\n  Jacobians is a substantial but self-contained project.\n* **The splitting distinguisher.** The attack recovers the secret by testing\n  whether the constructed abelian surface splits as a product of elliptic\n  curves. `kani_ker_inter_left` and `kani_ker_inter_right` are the local\n  statements behind this test; the global criterion needs the theory of\n  principal polarizations and the Weil pairing on `E\u2081 \u00d7 E\u2084`.\n\n## 4. Isotropy of the kernel and the polarization\n\nThe kernel `{(\u03c6^Q, \u03c8'Q)}` is isotropic for the product Weil pairing; this is\nwhat makes `F` an isogeny of *principally polarized* abelian surfaces. With a\nWeil pairing available (`Catalog/Cryptography/WeilPairingBLS.lean` has a start)\none can state and prove\n`e_N(\u03c6^Q, \u03c6^Q') \u00b7 e_N(\u03c8'Q, \u03c8'Q') = 1`, using `deg \u03c6 + deg \u03c8 = N`.\n\n## 5. The Deuring correspondence side\n\nThe mission statement also mentions the equivalence between computing an\nisogeny from `j`-invariants and the quaternion isogeny path problem. A\nformalization path: define the category of supersingular curves over `F_{p\u00b2}`\nwith its `\u2113`-isogeny graph, define maximal orders in `B_{p,\u221e}` with their\nconnecting ideals, and state the correspondence as an equivalence of groupoids.\nMathlib has quaternion algebras but no maximal-order theory, so this requires\nbuilding the arithmetic of quaternion orders first (norms, ideals, ideal\nclasses) \u2014 a large but very well-mapped project.\n\n## 6. Generalizing the diamond\n\nKani's lemma holds for any commutative square with `deg \u03c6 = deg \u03c6'`,\n`deg \u03c8 = deg \u03c8'`, not only for coprime degrees; only the *description* of the\nkernel needs `gcd(a,b) = 1`. The computational evidence in\n`ComputationalEvidence.md` shows that for `gcd(a,b) = d > 1` the kernel is\nstrictly larger than the graph. Determining the kernel in that case (it\ncontains `(E\u2081 \u00d7 E\u2084)[d]`-type contributions) would be a natural refinement of\n`mem_ker_kani_iff`.\n",
