    "description": "# Future directions\n\nState of the formalisation of *Gilbert's disc model conditioned on the square lattice*\n(one point per cell of `\u2124\u00b2`, two points joined when at distance `< R`), files\n`Catalog/Shared/GilbertLattice*.lean`:\n\n| radius | status |\n|---|---|\n| `R_min`  (some placement percolates)   | `1/3 \u2264 R_min \u2264 1/2` |\n| `R_conn` (some placement connects all) | `1/3 \u2264 R_conn \u2264 \u221a10/4 \u2248 0.7906 < 1` |\n| `R_full` (every placement connects all)| `R_full = \u221a5` **exact** |\n| aligned (constant-offset) family       | threshold **exactly** `1` |\n\n## 1. Close the gap for `R_min`\n\nNumerics and a covering heuristic both point to `R_min = 1/2`, the line placement being\noptimal.  The heuristic: a chain of `n` points at mutual distance `< R` lies on a\npolyline of length `< nR`; the `n` cells containing them lie in the `\u221a2`-neighbourhood\nof that polyline, of area `\u2264 2\u221a2 nR + O(1)`, whence `R \u2265 1/(2\u221a2) \u2248 0.3536`.  Two\nconcrete targets:\n\n* formalise the tube-covering bound to improve the current `1/3` to `1/(2\u221a2)`; this\n  needs the area of a tube around a polyline, which is not in Mathlib;\n* prove the sharp bound `R_min \u2265 1/2`.  A promising route is a *strip* argument: for\n  `R < 1/2` consecutive cells of a path are king-neighbours and each crossing of a\n  lattice line `x = K` forces both endpoints within `R` of the line; a counting argument\n  in a two-row strip gives `2` cells per column against the `1/R > 2` hops per column\n  needed to advance one column.  The difficulty is to rule out paths that drift in both\n  coordinates.\n\n## 2. Close the gap for `R_conn`\n\nThe zig-zag placement gives `R_conn \u2264 \u221a10/4`.  Simulated annealing over `2 \u00d7 2`\nperiodic placements stops at `\u2248 0.7877`, and larger periods did not do better, so\n`\u221a10/4` is likely close to optimal \u2014 but the true `R_conn` may be smaller if long-period\nor aperiodic placements help (rare \"bridges\" cost asymptotically nothing in density).\nTwo subproblems:\n\n* is `R_conn` attained by a periodic placement?\n* prove a lower bound for `R_conn` better than the inherited `1/3`; the numerics suggest\n  a bound of order `3/4`.\n\n## 3. Higher dimensions\n\nThe definitions generalise verbatim to `\u2124^d`.  Two points of edge-adjacent cells are at\ndistance `\u2264 \u221a(d + 3)`, so `R_full(d) \u2264 \u221a(d+3)`, and the diagonal-cut construction of\n`GilbertLatticeDiagonalCut.lean` gives `R_full(2) = \u221a5 = \u221a(2+3)`.  For `d \u2265 3` the plain\ndiagonal cut only yields `\u221a5`, so a genuinely `d`-dimensional cut is needed: is\n`R_full(d) = \u221a(d+3)`, or is the truth smaller?\n\n## 4. The arithmetic bridge\n\n`GilbertLatticeSumTwoSquares.lean` identifies the spectrum of squared edge lengths of\nthe aligned family with the nonzero norms of Gaussian integers, i.e. with sums of two\nsquares, and proves the geometric form of Fermat's two-square theorem, the\nmultiplicative (Brahmagupta\u2013Fibonacci) closure and the exact threshold `1` for aligned\nplacements.  Natural continuations:\n\n* **Gauss circle problem**: the degree of a vertex at radius `R` is\n  `#{(a,b) \u2208 \u2124\u00b2 \\ {0} : a\u00b2 + b\u00b2 < R\u00b2} = \u03c0R\u00b2 + O(R^{2/3})`.  Only the first case\n  (`1 < R \u2264 \u221a2`, degree `4`) is formalised\n  (`GilbertLattice.neighborSet_alignedConfig`); a general degree formula needs lattice\n  point counting, which Mathlib does not have.\n* **Jacobi's two-square theorem**: the number of edges from a fixed cell of squared\n  length exactly `n` is `r\u2082(n) = 4(d\u2081(n) \u2212 d\u2083(n))`.  This would turn the local geometry\n  of the model into a divisor-counting statement; `r\u2082` is not in Mathlib.\n* the spectrum submonoid `latticeSpectrumSubmonoid` is the image of the Gaussian norm\n  map; describing its generators (primes `2` and `p \u2261 1 [MOD 4]`, and squares of primes\n  `p \u2261 3 [MOD 4]`) would give a complete structure theorem.\n\n## 5. The probabilistic model\n\nEverything formalised so far is deterministic (statements about *all* placements, hence\nabout every realisation).  The genuinely probabilistic content of the paper \u2014 placing the\npoint of each cell uniformly and independently, and studying the a.s. percolation\nthreshold `R_c \u2208 (R_min, R_full)` \u2014 needs the product measure on `\u220f_{c \u2208 \u2124\u00b2} [0,1]\u00b2`, a\n`0-1` law for the event \"there is an infinite component\", and monotonicity in `R`.  The\nfirst two are within reach of Mathlib's `MeasureTheory.Measure.infinitePi` and the\nKolmogorov 0-1 law; the sharp threshold results are not.\n",
