    "description": "# Future directions\n\nThe new file `Catalog/Shared/RevisionHistoryMonoid.lean` completes the last-occurrence\nnormal-form programme: revision histories modulo extensional equivalence form a monoid\nisomorphic to the monoid of finitely supported partial sign assignments under override,\nthis monoid is a right regular band with trivial unit group, its action on belief states is\nfaithful, the normal form is the (unique up to permutation) shortest representative, and\nthe two local rewrite rules \u2014 swap adjacent revisions of distinct atoms, delete an\nimmediately superseded revision \u2014 are a *complete* presentation.\n\nNatural next steps.\n\n## 1. Green's relations and the band decomposition\n\n`support` is a surjective homomorphism onto the semilattice of finite atom sets,\n`mul_eq_right_iff` says `f * g = g \u2194 supp f \u2286 supp g`, and `mul_eq_right_and_iff` shows the\nfibres of `support` are right zero semigroups.  What remains is to define Green's `L`, `R`,\n`D` relations formally and prove that `PartialAssign Atom` is *the* semilattice\n`Finset Atom` of right zero bands `{\u00b11}^S`, placing the construction inside the standard\nclassification of bands.\n\n## 2. Infinite histories and limits\n\n`reviseSeq` is defined on finite lists.  For an infinite stream of revisions, the \"limit\nstate\" should be the set of literals whose atom is eventually never revised again, or whose\nlast revision exists.  Which streams have limits, and how does the finitary-open topology\nof `Novelty/DreamLogic.lean` interact with them?\n\n## 3. Priority and non-overwriting revisions\n\nReplace *last write wins* by a priority order on literals: a revision succeeds only if its\npriority is at least that of the recorded sign.  The resulting monoid should still be a\nband, but no longer right regular; the normal form becomes a maximal antichain computation.\n\n## 4. Quantitative revision\n\nWeight each revision by a confidence in `[0,1]` and replace the override product by a\nconvex combination.  The idempotent band structure degenerates to an affine dynamical\nsystem; the analogue of `card_partialAssign` becomes a volume computation.\n\n## 5. Decidability and complexity\n\n`histEq_iff_perm_normalForm` and `RevisionMonoid.instDecidableEq` already make equivalence\nof histories decidable.  The remaining work is quantitative: a verified linear-time\nnormalisation algorithm (the current one is quadratic, scanning the tail at each step) and\na formal complexity bound for it.\n",
