    "description": "# Future directions\n\nThe thread opened with two questions \u2014 the finite-key probability formula and\ncrossing-probability monotonicity \u2014 both of which are proved in\n`Catalog/Combinatorics/BernoulliThresholdCoupling.lean`.  Since then the work has\ngone into *depth*: a sprinkling/thinning calculus, the Bollob\u00e1s\u2013Thomason\nthreshold window, site-dependent densities with the Margulis\u2013Russo formula, a\nPoincar\u00e9 (variance\u2013influence) inequality proved by hybrid paths, its integrated\nodds-ratio form, a matching *reverse* Poincar\u00e9 inequality with the two-sided\nRusso differential inequality, and a self-contained `p`-biased Fourier analysis\non the discrete cube.\n\nThis cycle completed that Fourier development and used it to convert two of the\ninequalities into exact identities:\n\n* `Catalog/Combinatorics/BernoulliFourierParseval.lean` \u2014 the product rule for\n  the biased expectation, full orthogonality of the characters `\u03c8_S`, the\n  reproducing-kernel identity, **completeness** of the biased Fourier basis\n  (`fourier_expansion`), **Parseval** (`parseval`), the biased Plancherel\n  identity for Boolean functions (`fourier_weight_sum`), and the exact energy\n  decomposition `4P(1-P) = 4p(1-p) \u2211_v I_v\u00b2 + (level \u2265 2 energy)`, which exhibits\n  the `\u2113\u00b2` influence bound as the statement that a remainder is nonnegative and\n  pins down its equality case.\n* `Catalog/Combinatorics/BernoulliPoincareDefect.lean` \u2014 the identity\n  `\u2211_{S \u220b v} (p(1-p))^{|S|} \\hat f(S)\u00b2 = p(1-p) E[(D_v f)\u00b2]` (the biased form of\n  `Inf_v(f) = \u2211_{S \u220b v} \\hat f(S)\u00b2`), the resulting Efron\u2013Stein/Poincar\u00e9\n  inequality **for arbitrary functions** together with its exact defect\n  `\u2211_{S \u2260 \u2205} (|S| - 1)(p(1-p))^{|S|} \\hat f(S)\u00b2`, and, for increasing events, the\n  Fourier formula for the total influence, the Poincar\u00e9 inequality with exact\n  remainder, and its equality case (`poincare_eq_iff_degree_le_one`).\n\nThe following conjectures are the natural next targets; each is falsifiable at\nthe level of finite site sets, so each can be tested numerically \u2014 with the exact\nrational harness of `ComputationalEvidence.md` \u2014 before being attacked formally.\n\n## Conjecture 1 (two-point hypercontractivity and its tensorization)\n\nFor every `f : Bool \u2192 \u211d`, every `\u03c1 \u2208 [0,1]` with `\u03c1\u00b2 \u2264 p(1-p)/max(p,1-p)\u00b2`, and\nthe biased noise operator `T_\u03c1`, one has `\u2016T_\u03c1 f\u2016_4 \u2264 \u2016f\u2016_2`; and the same\ninequality holds for functions on `\u03b9 \u2192 Bool` with the same `\u03c1`, by tensorizing\nalong `expP_prod`.\n\n*The key insight is* that `expP_prod` \u2014 proved in this cycle \u2014 is exactly the\nstatement that the biased measure is a product measure, and tensorization of a\nhypercontractive estimate is nothing but repeated application of the product\nrule coordinate by coordinate; the two-point case is a single two-variable\npolynomial inequality accessible to `nlinarith` after clearing denominators.\n\n*Why now?* Hypercontractivity is the only missing ingredient between the present\ndevelopment and the whole KKL/Talagrand circle: the Fourier expansion, Parseval,\nthe level decomposition, and the influence identity are all formal already, and\neach of them is used verbatim in the classical proof.\n\n## Conjecture 2 (Talagrand's `L\u00b9`\u2013`L\u00b2` inequality on the cube)\n\nThere is an absolute constant `C > 0` such that for every increasing event `A` on\na finite site set `\u03b9`, writing `I_v = bernProb (1/2) (pivotalSet A v)`,\n\n`P (1 - P) \u2264 C \u00b7 \u2211_v I_v / Real.log (Real.exp 1 / I_v)`\n\n(with the convention that a site of zero influence contributes `0`).\n\n*The key insight is* that the present `\u2113\u00b2` bound and the defect identity already\ngive the two extreme cases \u2014 the `\u2113\u00b2` sum controls the variance exactly, and the\n`\u2113\u00b9` sum controls it with the crude Cauchy\u2013Schwarz loss \u2014 so Talagrand's\ninequality is precisely the interpolation obtained by splitting the Fourier\nexpansion at the level `log(1/I_v)` and applying Conjecture 1 to the tail.\n\n*Why now?* Talagrand's inequality implies the KKL influence bound\n`max_v I_v \u2265 c log|\u03b9| / |\u03b9|`, so proving it closes two conjectures of this thread\nat once; and its extremal family (tribes) is finite and directly testable\nagainst the rational harness.\n\n## Conjecture 3 (sharp threshold for transitive events)\n\nIf the automorphism group of `A \u2286 (\u03b9 \u2192 Bool)` acts transitively on `\u03b9`, then all\ninfluences are equal, and consequently the width of the density window on which\n`bernProb p A` moves from `\u03b5` to `1 - \u03b5` is at most `C(\u03b5) / log |\u03b9|`.\n\n*The key insight is* that under transitivity the Poincar\u00e9 defect identity forces\n`|\u03b9| \u00b7 I = 4 p(1-p) \u2211_v I_v \u2265 4 P(1-P)`, so a *lower* bound on the single common\ninfluence of the KKL type immediately upgrades Russo's formula to a logarithmic\nthreshold window \u2014 the symmetry replaces the union bound entirely.\n\n*Why now?* The equal-influence step is a two-line consequence of the formal\nstatement `siteEnergy_eq_influence`, since the site energies are manifestly\ninvariant under automorphisms; only the KKL lower bound (Conjecture 2) is\nmissing.\n\n## Conjecture 4 (BK / van den Berg\u2013Kesten inequality)\n\nFor increasing events `A`, `B` on a finite site set define disjoint occurrence\n`A \u25a1 B = {\u03b7 | \u2203 S T : Finset \u03b9, Disjoint S T \u2227 1_S \u2208 A \u2227 1_T \u2208 B \u2227 S \u222a T \u2286 open \u03b7}`.\nThen `bernProb p (A \u25a1 B) \u2264 bernProb p A * bernProb p B`.\n\n*The key insight is* that `sum_prod_pushforward` already provides the exact\nmechanism that BK needs \u2014 a coordinatewise duplication of the alphabet from\n`Bool` to `Bool \u00d7 Bool` \u2014 so the van den Berg\u2013Kesten induction can be phrased as\na sequence of pushforwards along `Bool \u00d7 Bool \u2192 Bool` rather than as a\nmeasure-theoretic coupling.\n\n*Why now?* The pushforward lemma, the pivotal calculus and the one-coordinate\ndecomposition `fun_decomp` built in this cycle are exactly the ingredients that\nthe classical proof manipulates informally; with the thinning law\n`bernProb_and_le` already formalized, BK is the last elementary correlation\ninequality of percolation still missing from the catalog.\n\n## Conjecture 5 (self-duality of bond crossings at density 1/2)\n\nFor bond percolation on the `n \u00d7 (n+1)` grid, the probability at `p = 1/2` that\nthe two short sides are joined by an open path equals exactly `1/2`; hence\n`thresholdDensity` of that crossing event at level `1/2` is exactly `1/2` for\nevery `n`.\n\n*The key insight is* that the complement of a horizontal open crossing is a\nvertical closed crossing of the dual rectangle, and the map `\u03c9 \u21a6 \u03c9\u1d9c` is\nweight-preserving at `p = 1/2` (`weight (1/2) \u03b7 = 2 ^ -|\u03b9|` for every `\u03b7`), so\nthe duality is a bijection between two events of equal weight rather than an\nanalytic statement.\n\n*Why now?* The catalog already has `bondThresholdConfig`, the bond form of the\nfinite-key formula, and `thresholdDensity`; the missing ingredient is a purely\ncombinatorial planar-duality lemma between open horizontal paths and closed\nvertical paths, which is finite and checkable by enumeration for `n \u2264 3`.\n",
